/**
@page cosmology_spectra Independent spectra and comparison metrics

@section spectra_direct Particle Fourier coefficients

For equal-mass particles and a nonzero integer mode,
@f[ \delta_{\boldsymbol n}=N_p^{-1}\sum_{p=1}^{N_p}
 \exp[-2\pi i\boldsymbol n\cdot\boldsymbol x_p/L]. @f]
This direct particle sum is independent of the simulation force mesh and is
used for the explicitly qualified low-mode band. A periodic translation
@f$\boldsymbol s@f$ multiplies the coefficient by
@f$\exp[-2\pi i\boldsymbol n\cdot\boldsymbol s/L]@f$; comparison code removes
that known phase analytically, without fitting a shift.

@section spectra_assignment Assignment windows and aliases

For an analysis grid N_A and a tensor-product order-p assignment,
@f[ W_p(\boldsymbol n)=\prod_d\operatorname{sinc}(n_d/N_A)^p,\qquad
\operatorname{sinc}(u)=\frac{\sin(\pi u)}{\pi u}. @f]
CIC has p=2; PCS (cubic B-spline) has p=4. Dividing a Fourier amplitude by Wp
corrects its assignment window. Power correction divides by Wp squared, not by
Wp. Interlacing two grids separated by half a cell removes odd alias images
after the phase alignment @cite sefusatti2016. It does not remove every alias.
The analysis grid and assignment kernel are separate from the simulation's
CIC force mesh. PCS higher-band measurements remain characterization unless
explicitly qualified against resolution and sampling controls.

@section spectra_shells Fourier shells and shot noise

Figure A11 uses radial shells @f$b=\lfloor|\boldsymbol n|+1/2\rfloor@f$.
The cutoff argument in the legacy shell estimator selects the largest rounded
bin index: bin b spans @f$b-1/2\le|\boldsymbol n|<b+1/2@f$.
Thus a last bin labelled 48 includes some modes above radial length 48, whereas
the generated initial-condition sphere is strictly @f$|\boldsymbol n|\le48@f$.
These two ceilings must not be conflated.
For R2C storage, the omitted conjugate partner is represented with multiplicity
2, except the stored zero and Nyquist planes whose multiplicity is 1.
The shell center and corrected power are weighted averages:
@f[ k_b=\frac{\sum_{\boldsymbol n\in b}w_{\boldsymbol n}(2\pi/L)|\boldsymbol n|}
{\sum_{\boldsymbol n\in b}w_{\boldsymbol n}},\qquad
P_b^{\rm raw}=\frac{\sum_{\boldsymbol n\in b}w_{\boldsymbol n}L^3
|\widehat\delta_{\boldsymbol n}|^2/|W_2(\boldsymbol n)|^2}
{\sum_{\boldsymbol n\in b}w_{\boldsymbol n}}. @f]
The plotted convention subtracts Poisson shot noise
@f[ P_b^{\rm sub}=P_b^{\rm raw}-L^3/N_p. @f]
The legacy `analyze_zeldovich_benchmark.cic_power` implementation uses its
module-level BoxSize in the raw power-volume factor. Its supported amplitude
contract requires the passed box_size to equal that fixed 168.75 Mpc/h value;
the local A11 runner satisfies this requirement. Changing the box argument
alone does not produce a correctly renormalized general-box spectrum.

This subtraction is a measurement convention, not proof that a displaced
lattice has independent Poisson sampling noise. Nonpositive corrected values
remain in the saved data and are omitted from log plots and corresponding
ratios; no arbitrary positive floor is substituted. A shell is a Fourier bin,
not a physical layer of particles in the box.

@section spectra_metrics Cross-code and refinement metrics

For displacement differences, use the minimum periodic image
@f$\Delta\boldsymbol x=\boldsymbol x_A-\boldsymbol x_B
-L\operatorname{rint}[(\boldsymbol x_A-\boldsymbol x_B)/L]@f$.
Vector RMS means @f$\sqrt{\langle|\boldsymbol v|^2\rangle}@f$; componentwise RMS
is different by a factor of sqrt(3) for an isotropic field. Every comparison
must state which convention and normalization it uses.
For two complex coefficient arrays A,B, common shell metrics include
@f[ \epsilon_c=\frac{\|A-B\|_2}{\|B\|_2},\quad
\epsilon_P=\frac{\sum|A|^2}{\sum|B|^2}-1,\quad
r=\frac{\operatorname{Re}\sum AB^*}
{\sqrt{\sum|A|^2\sum|B|^2}}. @f]
Check the calling validator for its chosen denominator, absolute versus signed
reporting and shell cuts. Small denominators and zero fields need explicit
handling; a finite reporting floor is not an accuracy qualification.

Successive refinements with step-size ratio 2 give an observed order
@f$p=\log_2(\|u_h-u_{h/2}\|/\|u_{h/2}-u_{h/4}\|)@f$
only above the declared numerical floor. These are successive-solution
differences, not errors against a continuum truth. Planar Zel'dovich evolution
is an analytical reference only before shell crossing.
The A11 lower panel uses
@f$100(P_A^{\rm sub}/P_{\rm FastPM}^{\rm sub}-1)@f$; the native TreePM force
and adaptive time schedule differ from both plain-PM implementations.
*/
